While Labeled Property Graphs (LPGs) and query languages like Cypher are popular for structured databases, the semantic stack (RDF, SPARQL, and SHACL) is uniquely suited as the abstraction layer for personal knowledge and agentic memory:
While both tools allow you to query your wiki metadata, they optimize for different workflows:
While traditionally non-semantic, tabular results from [SPARQL](SPARQL.md) SELECT queries are often serialized to CSV for easy manipulation in standard tooling.
Wiki CLI builds each article into HTML using a **page layout** file. Two levels apply:
Wiki CLI is intentionally batteries-included for semantic Markdown wikis: validation, formatting, querying, rendering, exporting, building, and local preview belong together. The boundary is not "minimal CLI only"; the boundary is "semantic wiki toolchain, not editor/app automation."
Wiki CLI processes files through two distinct pipelines. Files in `wiki.input` are classified as either **documents** (frontmatter parsed, IRI derived from file path, link-checked) or **raw RDF sources** (loaded directly via rdflib, no document processing):
Wiki CLI remains compatible with Obsidian-authored Markdown, but it should not duplicate Obsidian CLI's app-control surface.
Wiki page bodies are rendered without raw HTML passthrough; inline HTML tags in markdown are not emitted as live markup in built pages.
Wiki source management composes Git-backed Wiki/RDF sources into one semantic corpus without flattening ownership boundaries.
Wiki-specific query parameters (GET query string, or form body on `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` POST):
With `site.base_url: /wiki` and `site.url_style: dir`:
Without a configured layout file (or when the path is missing), every page is rendered with the built-in minimal fallback layout (`index.html`), which outputs plain, unstyled HTML containing only `<h1>` and content. It does not link any default CSS or assets and does not include a sidebar, tabs, infobox, table of contents, backlinks, or categories out-of-the-box.
Workflow assets (embed one template in full; substitute `CONFIG_PATH`, `SITE_BASE_URL`, `ARTIFACT_PATH` only):
XML itself is only a syntax. Specific vocabularies such as [RDF XML](RDF_XML.md), RSS, SVG, or custom application schemas define what the tags actually mean.
You can also add `<link rel="icon">`, `<link rel="apple-touch-icon">`, or other `<head>` tags directly in your custom layout file.
You can use GitHub `owner/repo` shorthand instead of a full URL — `EthanThatOneKid/solar-system-wiki` expands to `https://github.com/EthanThatOneKid/solar-system-wiki.git` automatically.
You may omit `type` when `graph.implicit_types` is set in `wiki.yaml` (see [Wiki Configuration](Wiki_Configuration.md)); the CLI applies those CURIEs at graph build time. Explicit `type` in frontmatter always wins under `implicit_types_policy: fallback`.
Zero-install (no global install):
[![skills.sh](https://skills.sh/b/wazootech/wiki)](https://skills.sh/wazootech/wiki)
[Jeff Kazzee](https://github.com/Jeff-Kazzee) builds zero-dependency tooling for **agent-native workspaces**: typed Markdown knowledge graphs, validation gates, self-improving agent loops, and graph-aware review by blast radius. His tools share a design law — the framework must cost almost nothing to load, or it steals the context the work needs.
[Obsidian](https://obsidian.md/) is a local-first, markdown-based [personal knowledge](Personal_Knowledge.md) management application.
[Procedural Knowledge](Procedural_Knowledge.md) for coding agents lives in the Wiki CLI repository under `skills/`. The **`wiki`** skill routes operational workflows to focused references, including code-wiki sync. Skills are **not** wiki pages — do not add `skills/` to `wiki.input`.
[Vivary](https://vivary.vercel.app/) is a **standard plus scaffolder for agent-native workspaces**: `create-vivary` seeds a thin governed `.vivary/` contract that agents read first, a typed knowledge graph of Markdown records, verification receipts, and deliberate human gates — plain Markdown and TOML, any editor, any agent runtime, no lock-in.
